A||N||5||Coop88||88 e-brake ||||5||
Z||000000||Coop88||11-16-2000||09:20 PM||||Hi. I did some searching and didn't see this come up before. I replaced the rear calipers and the brake cables back in March when I bought my formula. I used liqiud graphite on all 3 cables to lube them up. I can activate the e-brake fine, but the driver's rear caliper won't fully disengage. It did this right after installation, too. When I installed everything, I set the e-brake kinda loose, and the caliper would disengage. Recently I tightened them up, forgetting about the caliper. Now I have to reach up and pull the driver's rear caliper lever back by hand, or I can pull the adjuster underneath the car to the driver's side and it will disenguage. My questions are:<BR>1.What should I re-lube my cables with?<BR>2.Is there some other adjustment that I need to do, other than at the place where the 3 cables meet?<BR>Any info is appreciated. Thanks<BR>Sorry this is so long.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||11-16-2000||11:34 PM||||HI Coop,<P>You might try another return spring, the one on it might not be strong enough.<BR>Check also for binding of the lever itself on the caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Coop88||11-17-2000||09:08 AM||||I replaced both springs when I installed the new calipers. I will check that lever, though. Should I just use some more liquid graphite on my cables?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-17-2000||07:51 PM||||This sounds like the caliper may be hanging up some as well. take off the cable and spring on each side and compair how the arm feels when you move it by hand. It's not the best test but it may help.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Coop88||11-19-2000||03:33 PM||||I went ahead and put some stronger springs on the calipers. The caliper isn't sticking. I can work the cables by hand ok. Right now I can operate the brake and release it alright. I can't tighten it anymore or else it won't release on the driver's side. I guess I'm alright until I need to park on a hill. I can push the car around while the brake is activated. I just have a couple questions.<BR>1. How much 'play' should there be in the brake lever (inside car)? I can lift mine about 2-3 inches before I feel any resistence.<BR>2. When the brake is activated, should the levers on the calipers be in the same position? My passenger side caliper activates way more than the drivers side.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||11-19-2000||06:40 PM||||Mine's just the opposite, my passenger doesnt seem to engage as much as the driver.  But as long as it holds, you should be fine.<BR>   I belive the adjustment for the tension is after about 5-6 clicks something should be holding.  <BR>   As for lubrication of the cables, I have heard of a tool that you can hook on to a grease gun and force a lubricant all the way in the cable, not just near the ends of them.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
